308:          <h3>Where the leverage lives in <code>programmable/</code></h3>
323:        <div class="move move--try">
324:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — leverage ledger</p>
325:          <h3>Measure attention, not impressiveness</h3>
336:        <div class="move move--fail">
337:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
338:          <h3>Failure: confusing more output with more leverage</h3>
343:        <div class="move move--current">
344:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
345:          <h3>Period detail changes; leverage does not</h3>
346:          <aside class="current-practice">
347:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
349:            <p class="current-practice__source">Use today’s tool, but grade it with the same question: how much reliable outcome does one unit of attention purchase?</p>
353:        <div class="move move--check">
354:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
355:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for leverage and mission">
356:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why does Dan say the “10x” number is arbitrary?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the multiplier comes from the leverage point you find. It could be 2x, 10x, or 100x; it does not come from a fixed amount of greater effort.</p></details></div>
357:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What makes the programmable demo leverage rather than just automation theater?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow can be recreated repeatedly from a known state with low additional human attention, and it returns evidence through the report.</p></details></div>
358:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does a commander of compute decide?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The target, context, constraints, bounded tools, success evidence, and timing of a system that performs engineering work.</p></details></div>
362:        <div class="move move--teach">
363:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
364:          <div class="teach-back">
366:            <p class="fine">Pass if you mention repetition, marginal attention, evidence, and a judgment boundary.</p>
371:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stop">
372:        <span class="section-number">03</span>
373:        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
374:        <p class="section-lead">The provocation is precise: stop <em>hand-typing</em> application code. Keep generating code. Keep understanding code. Move your scarce cycles to planning, reviewing, and building closed loops.</p>
376:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
381:        <div class="move move--hook">
382:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
383:          <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
389:        <div class="move move--picture">
390:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — reallocate the engineering cycle</p>
391:          <h3>Do not remove engineering; move it around the code</h3>
392:          <figure class="diagram">
393:            <div class="diagram__canvas">
422:        <div class="move move--story">
423:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
424:          <h3>The tactic is said twice because the easy interpretation is wrong</h3>
426:          <figure class="frame-card">
428:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:54</span>The headline is intentionally uncomfortable. Read the object of the verb carefully: stop personally <em>typing</em> the code, not producing or understanding it.</figcaption>
433:        <div class="move move--name">
434:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
435:          <h3>Stop coding ≠ stop engineering</h3>
436:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
437:            <p class="callout__label">Exact distinction</p>
449:        <div class="move move--example">
450:          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
451:          <h3>Zero application-code keystrokes in TAC-1</h3>
457:          <aside class="callout callout--why">
458:            <p class="callout__label">Why the constraint matters</p>
463:        <div class="move move--try">
464:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — repair without reclaiming the keyboard</p>
465:          <h3>Use the next imperfection as the exercise</h3>
476:        <div class="move move--fail">
477:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
478:          <h3>Two opposite misunderstandings</h3>
479:          <div class="comparison">
480:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No more code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> code remains a primary asset that produces features and products. TAC expects you to generate more of it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> say “stop hand-typing” every time you teach the tactic.</p></div></article>
481:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No need to understand code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> faster generation magnifies the cost of weak review and weak system judgment.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move reading, execution, tests, and diff review into explicit pass criteria.</p></div></article>
486:        <div class="move move--current">
487:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
488:          <h3>The tactic has a public companion</h3>
489:          <aside class="current-practice">
490:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current-practice video · plan-approved</p>
492:            <p class="current-practice__source">Metadata should be rechecked at publication; the ID is supplied by the locked extract.</p>
494:          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
495:            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2TIXl2rlA6Q">
496:              <span class="video-card__type">Operationalizes the tactic</span>
499:              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
504:        <div class="move move--check">
505:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
506:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
507:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly stops when you follow Tactic #1?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Your hand-typing of application code. Code generation and code understanding continue.</p></details></div>
508:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Where do the freed engineering cycles go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning, reviewing, and creating closed-loop structures.</p></details></div>
509:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
510:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
514:        <div class="move move--teach">
515:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
516:          <div class="teach-back">
518:            <p class="fine">Pass if the learner could act differently on their next task.</p>
523:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
524:        <span class="section-number">04</span>
525:        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
526:        <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>
528:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
533:        <div class="move move--hook">
534:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
535:          <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>
541:        <div class="move move--picture">
542:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the Core Four visual</p>
543:          <h3>Four interacting primitives, one newly emphasized dimension</h3>
544:          <figure class="diagram">
545:            <div class="diagram__canvas">
546:              <div class="core-four" role="img" aria-label="Core Four: context, model, prompt, and tools, with tools highlighted as the added phase-two dimension">
547:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Context</b><p>What the model can see: repo, files, settings, current state.</p></div>
548:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Model</b><p>The reasoning and generation capacity doing the work.</p></div>
549:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Prompt</b><p>The outcome, constraints, ordered actions, and report request.</p></div>
550:                <div class="core-four__cell core-four__cell--tools"><b>Tools</b><p>The bounded actions the agent can execute in a reliable chain.</p></div>
551:                <span class="core-four__plus">4</span>
556:          <div class="concept-strip">
557:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Context</b><p>“Can it see what matters?”</p></div>
558:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Model</b><p>“Can it reason through this?”</p></div>
559:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Prompt</b><p>“Does it know the outcome and checks?”</p></div>
560:            <div class="concept-strip__item concept-strip__item--new"><b>Tools</b><p>“Can it take the required actions?”</p></div>
