{
  "name": "The Verifier Agent — spend tokens to save time; attack the review constraint with an agent that verifies agents",
  "one_liner": "Stop hand-checking every '✅ done.' Put a second, read-only agent behind your builder that decomposes its work into atomic claims, proves each one deterministically, and re-prompts the builder — unprompted — when a claim fails. You trade 2–5× the tokens for the one thing tokens can't buy back: your time.",
  "why_uncomfortable": "By the token-optimizer's math it looks pure waste — you double or 5× your spend on an agent that writes no code and often just says 'looks good.' Worse, you deliberately lock yourself OUT of your own verifier: its input bar is disabled, you cannot type at it, you cannot vibe-code a one-off fix. When it misses something, the only available move is to edit the persona, the script, or the prompt template — you are forced to template your engineering every single time. It removes the lowest-hanging fruit of the generative-AI age (fire a one-off prompt and move on) from the menu, and it pushes you out of the interactive back-and-forth loop most engineers are comfortable living in. Dan's framing: the discomfort is the mechanism."
}
"Cross the widening gap between the two camps of 2026 engineers. Camp A is stuck prompting back and forth with a single agent — in 2026 that will feel like hand-typing code did in 2025, and they will be far behind. Camp B builds multi-agent systems that scale: agents coordinating with agents, code stacked with agents to run longer threads of work. The verifier agent is the simplest, highest-leverage entry into Camp B — a brutally simple two-agent system that is the rare pattern which compounds on BOTH trust and scale at once. You are not building your application anymore; you are building the agentic system that builds and checks the application. '2026 is the year of trust': do you trust your agents to do what you ask — and at what scale?"
null
