55:        <p class="hero__dek">Stop hand-checking every “done.” Put a second, input-locked, read-only agent behind the builder. It wakes on every stop event, turns the builder’s claims into atomic propositions, proves each against actual state, and sends one concrete correction back when proof fails.</p>
62:        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to trace the builder/verifier lifecycle without calling it delegation, decompose a bundled claim into deterministic checks, explain the token-for-time trade, defend the three read-only layers, read the Report as a control surface, improve a verifier through its files instead of a one-off prompt, and reproduce the SQLite fail→feedback→repair→PERFECT loop in the real course codebase.</p>
72:            <span>Observes. Reads the turn slice, proves claims, and prompts back only on failure.</span>
103:        <p class="section-lead">The builder says it created the table, added the foreign key, applied the migration, and checked the result. That sentence is not evidence. It is a bundle of claims that has quietly transferred a new job to you: prove that every part is true.</p>
111:          <p><strong>Spend tokens to save time.</strong> Use a second, different, read-only agent to prove the builder’s atomic claims. Trade 2–5× the tokens for review hours you do not have to spend yourself.</p>
117:            <div class="comparison__head"><h3>Not model delegation</h3><p>The builder model does not decide to call a verifier or coordinate its proof work.</p></div>
121:            <div class="comparison__head"><h3>A top-down observer</h3><p>The verifier is attached to the builder’s lifecycle and proof surface.</p></div>
129:          <li><strong>Decompose proof</strong>Turn one confident summary into independently testable atoms with domain-appropriate evidence for each.</li>
131:          <li><strong>Improve the class</strong>Convert an unverifiable gap into a durable persona, script, or template improvement without typing at the verifier.</li>
132:          <li><strong>Operate the real loop</strong>Use <code>just vsql</code> and the shipped files to catch a planted schema defect and collect proof of automatic repair.</li>
141:        <p>Each of the nine ideas follows the same active sequence: feel the failure, see the system, replay a frame-backed moment, name the concept, inspect the course source, attempt a small task, diagnose a tempting error, update the idea for current practice, retrieve it from memory, and teach it back. Do the “You try” prompt before opening the retrieval answers. In the lab, save exact commands and outputs. “The verifier looked green” is a weak claim; “<code>atomic_claims_failed: 0</code> followed a failed FK atom proven by <code>PRAGMA foreign_key_list</code>” is inspectable evidence.</p>
153:          <p>That setup also lets the builder choose the story the reviewer sees. If you paste only the summary, the reviewer receives the claims but not the tool calls, results, and actual repository state behind them. The second agent may sound independent while inheriting the builder’s framing. More chat is not automatically more proof.</p>
183:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">4:06</span><code>verifier_prompt ✓ ack: prompt delivered to builder</code> closes the loop. The left side proves and corrects; the right side rebuilds.</figcaption>
277:          <p>Skimming the answer does not remove that obligation. It only replaces verification with confidence in fluent prose. The more work the agent performs per turn, the more claims can hide behind a compact summary. Throughput rises while the human’s proof capacity stays fixed.</p>
284:            <article><h3>Planning</h3><p>Define intent, scope, constraints, sequence, and success evidence before compute runs.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> Did we specify the right outcome and proof?</p></article>
287:          <p>The verifier does not remove planning. It makes reviewing parallel and repeatable, then improves planning indirectly: every claim it cannot verify becomes a request for a better fixture, oracle, script command, or specification next time.</p>
298:          <p>The distinction is subtle but important. The verifier is not “another opinion about the image.” The persona turned a reusable review judgment—limit distinct text blocks—into a rule. The agent applied that rule to the bytes that were produced. The Report tells the operator what was proven, what was not, and what happened next.</p>
306:            <p><strong>Planning</strong> determines what the system should do. <strong>Reviewing</strong> determines whether the actual result deserves trust. The verifier improves the review constraint by executing a domain-specific proof routine on every completed turn.</p>
308:          <p>Review in this system is a verb, not a feeling. It consists of decomposing claims, selecting checks, running them against actual state, recording evidence, and admitting what remains unknown. SQLite checks can be deterministic tool outputs; image checks are model-mediated judgments grounded in the actual pixels. A green status is the output of that process; it is not a substitute for the process.</p>
315:          <p>That list is valuable because it is both domain-specific and stable. The builder may phrase its summary differently on every turn, but the verifier maps claims to the same deterministic checks. The proof routine does not depend on the operator remembering which command to run after a long generation session.</p>
321:          <h3>Build a proof checklist from one sentence</h3>
330:          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> force every verified finding to include an oracle. In the course persona, a missing deterministic output must become <code>unsure</code>, not verified. That rule protects you from a reviewer that merely agrees with the builder’s story.</p>
348:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Which constraint does the verifier directly attack?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Reviewing. It executes repeatable proof checks after every builder turn.</p></details></div>
349:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the builder’s final answer not proof?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is the builder’s own assertion about its work. Proof requires an independent check against actual state and the output of that check.</p></details></div>
358:            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation includes planning, reviewing, actual state, and a deterministic oracle.</p>
366:        <p class="section-lead">A verifier is only as trustworthy as the granularity of its claims. “Done” is too large to prove. Each atom needs one unambiguous truth value and one evidence path.</p>
390:          <h3>Different domain, same proof discipline</h3>
391:          <p>Dan boots <code>just vsql</code> and asks the builder to find the SQLite databases and break down their tables, columns, and relationships. That request sounds like one inspection task. The verifier turns it into sixteen claims and proves them individually with <code>PRAGMA</code> queries, row counts, index checks, foreign-key checks, and an integrity result.</p>
394:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">19:40</span>Sixteen independently proven atoms, not one “database inspection passed.” The same frame also reveals the deliberate compute disparity between builder and verifier.</figcaption>
396:          <p>The image example reaches the same endpoint through a non-deterministic oracle: the vision-capable verifier checks the actual pixels against seven visual claims. The mechanism is stable across domains even when the check changes—decompose, choose an oracle, observe, record, decide.</p>
399:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">7:00</span>The repaired image closes at 7/7 with no feedback required. A PERFECT label means every atom was proven with zero gaps in that cycle.</figcaption>
408:            <p><strong>Atomic claim:</strong> the smallest proposition in the request or builder result that can be independently proven true, proven false, or declared unverifiable with the available oracle.</p>
447:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · model-agnostic proof</p>
448:            <p>The video uses GLM 5.1 for SQLite and GPT-5.5 for images; the checked-in personas now declare <code>openai/gpt-5.5</code>. The model line moved. The proof discipline did not: atomic claims, domain oracle, exact evidence, honest gaps.</p>
457:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What three outcomes can an atom receive?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Proven true, proven false, or explicitly unverifiable with the available oracle.</p></details></div>
466:            <p>A teammate complains that sixteen findings are noisier than one green check. Explain why the extra rows are not verbosity but preserved proof boundaries. Use one table claim and one image readability claim to show that the oracle can differ while the atomic method stays the same.</p>
489:            <div class="token-balance__bar"><b>Verifier · often 2–5×</b><span>Re-read, decompose, run proof checks, report, and correct</span></div>
500:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">2:58</span>Compute is already layered: the builder model authors a prompt for GPT Image 2. The verifier then adds another proof pass. The system spends deliberately.</figcaption>
518:          <p>The course README states the trade directly: the verifier often burns 2–5× the builder’s tokens and gives a second perspective plus deterministic checks. Suppose a SQLite turn reports tables, columns, foreign keys, indexes, counts, and integrity. Without the verifier, the alternative is not zero cost. It is the operator reconstructing the same checklist, running the same queries, comparing the same outputs, and deciding which discrepancy should become a correction.</p>
539:          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> optimize total delivery cost under a trust requirement. Make review time visible, make verifier spend visible, and compare them. The frame’s context percentages are not a scoreboard to minimize; they are proof that compute was allocated to checking.</p>
555:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What two resources are exchanged?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Additional model compute/tokens are exchanged for reduced human review time and increased proof.</p></details></div>
574:        <p class="section-lead">Dan’s claim is not merely that a verifier improves quality. It is that this pattern occupies a rare middle ground: the system does more work while also attaching more evidence to the result.</p>
585:          <h3>More work + more proof</h3>
722:            <p><strong>Template your engineering:</strong> encode a discovered check or correction in the persona, domain script, or cycle template so the next run inherits the improvement without relying on human memory.</p>
744:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — place the improvement</p>
775:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why is the input lock a feature?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It removes one-off operator prompting and forces improvements into durable context, persona, script, or template surfaces.</p></details></div>
777:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Is image text-block verification deterministic?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. The rule is explicit, but a vision-capable model judges the actual pixels and counts blocks. Treat it as model-mediated evidence.</p></details></div>
826:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — read-only by architecture</p>
854:          <p>Mutation keywords are rejected with structured JSON. PRAGMA names must be in an explicit introspection allowlist. One caveat remains: any command beginning with <code>.</code> is passed to <code>sqlite3 -readonly</code>; the script does not define a dot-command allowlist. The CLI’s <code>-readonly</code> flag protects database opening, but the source does not prove that every possible dot-command is filesystem-side-effect free.</p>
859:          <h3>Write what each layer proves and does not prove</h3>
883:            <p class="current-practice__source">The lesson teases a later agentic-security deep dive, but no source-approved video ID exists yet.</p>
903:            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation is still useful after removing the words “impossible,” “absolute,” and “sandbox.”</p>
910:        <h2 class="section-title">Turn every unverifiable gap into the next improvement</h2>
911:        <p class="section-lead">The Report does more than declare a verdict. Its two most valuable questions expose what the current verifier cannot prove and what evidence surface must be added next.</p>
917:          <p>Honest uncertainty feels less satisfying than a verdict, but it is better engineering data. A verifier that can say exactly why it could not check something has identified the next system improvement for you.</p>
940:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the architecture contains its own improvement loop</p>
957:          <div class="flywheel" aria-label="Verifier improvement flywheel">
980:          <p>Notice that FEEDBACK is not the worst state. It means the verifier found a failed atom and successfully used <code>verifier_prompt</code>. FAILED means the verifier could not perform the needed verification at all. The ladder distinguishes “the work is wrong and the loop is functioning” from “the proof system lacks an oracle.”</p>
985:          <h3>Do not invent the improvement before the Report</h3>
993:          <p>Pass if the before/after pair proves a class improvement. A one-off operator instruction does not count.</p>
999:          <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> it pressures the verifier to guess and discards the exact information needed to improve the system. The next run encounters the same blind spot.</p>
1008:            <p>A SQLite atom may close with a deterministic PRAGMA row. An image atom closes with model-mediated inspection of actual pixels. Do not flatten both into the word “proof” without naming the evidence type. The Report contract can stay stable while confidence reflects the domain’s oracle.</p>
1016:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Which two Report questions drive improvement?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>“What could you not verify?” and “What do you need from me to verify this next time?”</p></details></div>
1076:          <p>“Different model” is a design for independent perspective, not proof that two named models will always catch one another’s errors. The engineering value is role and context separation plus domain evidence. Evaluate the actual pair; do not turn model diversity into superstition.</p>
1114:          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> choose a focused domain, define its evidence surface, give the verifier only the role it needs, and place model choice inside that system. Add a new specialized persona when the proof problem changes; do not silently broaden the old one.</p>
1150:        <p class="section-lead">The extract approves two companion videos because Dan names the larger systems in the lesson. They extend the map; they do not replace the two-agent primitive or the course codebase lab.</p>
1168:          <p>Both IDs and titles come from the locked lesson extract. Direct metadata resolution was unavailable in this build environment, so re-check the links at publication. The teased agentic-security video has no approved ID and is intentionally omitted.</p>
1227:          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 2:</strong> submit a table with columns “mechanism,” “blocks,” “does not prove,” and “hardening.” Accuracy is worth more than a perfect-sounding guarantee.</div>
1234:            <li><strong>Flywheel option:</strong> use a real “could not verify” entry, add the smallest persona/script/template capability, and prove the same gap closes next run.</li>
1235:            <li><strong>Image option:</strong> run <code>just vimg</code>, generate an image clearly above ten text blocks, observe model-mediated readability feedback, change <code>MAX_TEXT_ELEMENTS</code> to six, and generate a case clearly above six. Do not grade an exact boundary count as deterministic.</li>
1249:            <tr><td>Compounding improvement / teach-back</td><td>Missing</td><td>One-off prompt</td><td>Change made</td><td>Rerun evidence</td><td>Class improvement + complete teach-back</td></tr>
1276:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">A Report says “database valid” because <code>PRAGMA integrity_check</code> returned <code>ok</code>. What is missing?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Integrity alone does not prove requested primary keys, foreign-key targets, columns, defaults, or indexes. Those claims need separate atoms and checks.</p></details></div>
1277:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">An image verifier says a diagram has seven text blocks. What kind of evidence is that?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A model-mediated visual judgment grounded in the actual image, not a deterministic parser result.</p></details></div>
1311:          <div><dt>Domain-appropriate evidence</dt><dd>The observation that settles an atom in its domain: deterministic query/test output where available, or model-mediated inspection grounded in the artifact for visual claims.</dd></div>
1315:          <div><dt>Templated engineering</dt><dd>Encoding a recurring verification improvement into the persona, checker script, or cycle template rather than a one-off chat instruction.</dd></div>
1320:          <div><dt>Core Four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools: the durable surfaces to improve when the verifier misses.</dd></div>
        <a href="../index.html">← Book hub</a>
        <p>MA-4 · The Pi Verifier Agent</p>
        <span>End of lesson 18</span>
      </footer>

      <script>
        (() => {
          const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
          const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
          const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
          let scheduled = false;

          const updateProgress = () => {
            const root = document.documentElement;
            const range = Math.max(1, root.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight);
            const value = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / range));
            const percent = Math.round(value * 100);
            topBar.style.width = `${percent}%`;
            railBar.style.width = `${percent}%`;
            label.textContent = `${percent}%`;
            scheduled = false;
          };

          const schedule = () => {
            if (!scheduled) {
              scheduled = true;
              window.requestAnimationFrame(updateProgress);
            }
          };

          window.addEventListener('scroll', schedule, { passive: true });
          window.addEventListener('resize', schedule);
          updateProgress();

          const navLinks = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
          const sectionIds = new Set(navLinks.map((link) => link.getAttribute('href').slice(1)));
          const sections = [...document.querySelectorAll('main section[id]')]
            .filter((section) => sectionIds.has(section.id));

          const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
            const visible = entries
              .filter((entry) => entry.isIntersecting)
              .sort((a, b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
            if (!visible) return;
            navLinks.forEach((link) => {
              if (link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`) {
                link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
              } else {
                link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
              }
            });
          }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, 0.1, 0.5] });

          sections.forEach((section) => observer.observe(section));
        })();
      </script>
    </main>
  </div>
</body>
</html>

diff --git a/IndyDevDan/book/vol-3-ma/18-verifier-agent.assets.css b/IndyDevDan/book/vol-3-ma/18-verifier-agent.assets.css
new file mode 100644
