+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section chapter-section--wide" id="lab">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Graded lab · Prove the verifier, then audit it</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Run only inside <code>agentic-horizon-extras/the-verifier-agent-system</code>. The required runtime drill plants a SQLite defect, watches the verifier drive a repair, and collects atomic evidence. The required static audit then checks whether the security prose matches the actual gate.</p>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Prime directive</p>
+          <p>Never type an ordinary instruction into the verifier window. All operator prompts go to the builder. Every verifier behavior change belongs in <code>.pi/verifier/agents/</code>, <code>.pi/verifier/scripts/</code>, or <code>.pi/verifier/prompts/verify_on_stop.md</code>.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <div class="lab-phase">
+          <span class="lab-phase__time">PHASE 0 · 15 MIN · STATIC + SETUP</span>
+          <h3>Establish the real authority and prerequisites</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Open the course repository. Run <code>/install</code> from the supplied Claude command or follow the README’s manual installation. The course checks Node 20+, Pi, tmux, uv, Python 3.11+, sqlite3, just, package installation, persona/script validity, provider-key presence, and <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code>.</li>
+            <li>Ensure <code>.pi/verifier/scripts/verify_sqlite.py</code> and <code>verify_python.py</code> are executable.</li>
+            <li>Add the provider key required by your current persona to a project-local <code>.env</code>. Confirm presence without printing the value.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>justfile</code>, <code>verify_sqlite.md</code>, <code>verifier.ts</code> lines 499–543, and <code>verify_sqlite.py</code> lines 18–93 and 256–293.</li>
+            <li>Write a four-row preflight: persona tools, bash predicate, SQL/PRAGMA routing, and remaining caveat. Do not copy the README’s stronger wording without reconciling it to code.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 0:</strong> state the intended three SQLite layers and the current <code>startsWith</code> / dot-command limitations without notes.</div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="lab-phase">
+          <span class="lab-phase__time">PHASE 1 · 25–35 MIN · REQUIRED RUNTIME</span>
+          <h3>Catch a planted SQLite defect and watch the repair loop close</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>The course tree does not ship the video’s runtime databases. Ask the <strong>builder</strong> to create <code>tmp/mock_shop.db</code> with <code>users</code>, <code>products</code>, and <code>orders</code>. Deliberately choose one defect from the extract: omit the <code>orders</code> primary key, or point <code>orders.user_id</code> at a mistyped table name such as <code>user</code> instead of <code>users</code>.</li>
+            <li>Run <code>just vsql</code>. If already inside tmux, look for a sibling window; otherwise the launcher attempts a new terminal window attached to a detached verifier tmux session.</li>
+            <li>Confirm the verifier’s ordinary input is locked and the builder shows a connected verifier.</li>
+            <li>Prompt the builder: <em>“Inspect the schema of tmp/mock_shop.db and confirm every table has a primary key and every foreign key references a real table and column.”</em></li>
+            <li>Do not touch the verifier. Observe the stop event, bounded turn-slice read, atom list, and checker calls.</li>
+            <li>Require separate evidence for <code>.schema orders</code>, <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders)</code>, <code>PRAGMA foreign_key_list(orders)</code>, existence of each referenced table/column, and <code>PRAGMA integrity_check</code>. Do not let <code>integrity_check</code> stand in for PK/FK correctness.</li>
+            <li>Capture the first <code>STATUS: failed</code> / <code>CONFIDENCE: FEEDBACK</code> Report, the exact failed atom, the observed output, the concrete <code>verifier_prompt</code>, and the builder repair.</li>
+            <li>Capture the next cycle with <code>atomic_claims_failed: 0</code>. PERFECT requires every atom checked with no gaps and no further correction.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="evidence-pack">
+            <div><strong>Artifact A</strong><p>Failing Report with one circled atom and exact evidence.</p></div>
+            <div><strong>Artifact B</strong><p><code>verifier_prompt</code> acknowledgement and builder repair turn.</p></div>
+            <div><strong>Artifact C</strong><p>Passing Report showing zero failed atoms and all gaps disclosed.</p></div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 1:</strong> explain why a mistyped FK target may require both <code>foreign_key_list</code> and a separate target-existence check, especially when the table contains no data.</div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="lab-phase">
+          <span class="lab-phase__time">PHASE 2 · 15–20 MIN · REQUIRED STATIC AUDIT</span>
+          <h3>Verify the verifier’s security claim</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Copy the exact <code>tools:</code> list from <code>verify_sqlite.md</code> and identify absent mutation tools.</li>
+            <li>Copy the exact bash acceptance predicate from <code>verifier.ts</code>. Explain why string-prefix recognition is weaker than fixed executable dispatch.</li>
+            <li>Trace a raw <code>INSERT</code> through <code>verify_sqlite.py</code>. Record that the router rejects it before the SQLite connection; do not claim this run demonstrated connection-level refusal.</li>
+            <li>Trace an allowlisted PRAGMA and record the read-only URI connection.</li>
+            <li>Trace a dot-command and record that it passes to <code>sqlite3 -readonly</code> without a script-side dot-command allowlist.</li>
+            <li>Write one hardening proposal: fixed executable/argv dispatch, explicit dot-command allowlist, or both.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 2:</strong> submit a table with columns “mechanism,” “blocks,” “does not prove,” and “hardening.” Accuracy is worth more than a perfect-sounding guarantee.</div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="lab-phase">
+          <span class="lab-phase__time">PHASE 3 · 20 MIN · CHOOSE ONE</span>
+          <h3>Extend the system without leaving the course repository</h3>
+          <ul>
+            <li><strong>Flywheel option:</strong> use a real “could not verify” entry, add the smallest persona/script/template capability, and prove the same gap closes next run.</li>
+            <li><strong>Image option:</strong> run <code>just vimg</code>, generate an image clearly above ten text blocks, observe model-mediated readability feedback, change <code>MAX_TEXT_ELEMENTS</code> to six, and generate a case clearly above six. Do not grade an exact boundary count as deterministic.</li>
+            <li><strong>Model option:</strong> change only the SQLite persona’s <code>model:</code>, rerun the same fixture and prompt, and compare atom coverage, gaps, corrections, duration, and spend.</li>
+            <li><strong>Domain-authoring option:</strong> clone the two-file SQLite pattern into <code>verify_&lt;domain&gt;.md</code> and <code>verify_&lt;domain&gt;.py</code>, preserve the structured rejection and Report contracts, and boot with <code>--verifier-agent verify_&lt;domain&gt;</code>. Keep the work inside the course repo.</li>
+          </ul>
+        </div>
+
+        <h3>Rubric · 20 points</h3>
+        <table class="scorecard">
+          <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Prime directive and lifecycle trace</td><td>Typed at verifier / no trace</td><td>Partial</td><td>Trigger known</td><td>Accurate path</td><td>Accurate path + protocol nuance</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Atomic evidence</td><td>Summary only</td><td>Coarse pass</td><td>Some atoms</td><td>All required atoms</td><td>All atoms + gaps + exact outputs</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Automatic correction loop</td><td>Not shown</td><td>Manual repair</td><td>Failure only</td><td>Feedback + repair</td><td>Fail→feedback→repair→zero failures</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Security accuracy</td><td>Prompt-only claim</td><td>Three-layer slogan</td><td>Mechanisms named</td><td>Limits named</td><td>Limits + precise hardening proposal</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Compounding improvement / teach-back</td><td>Missing</td><td>One-off prompt</td><td>Change made</td><td>Rerun evidence</td><td>Class improvement + complete teach-back</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+        <aside class="callout callout--why"><p class="callout__label">Pass standard</p><p><strong>16/20 minimum</strong>, with no zero in lifecycle, atomic evidence, or security accuracy. A learner cannot compensate for an unsafe or unverified loop with polished prose.</p></aside>
+
+        <h3>Troubleshooting grounded in the course</h3>
+        <div class="comparison">
+          <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>No second window</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Inside tmux, check sibling windows. Outside tmux, inspect the builder’s surfaced spawn error. Common causes in the extract are missing dependencies or an invalid persona model id. Use <code>just clean</code> for stale verifier tmux sessions, sockets, and breadcrumbs.</p></div></article>
+          <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Zero claims</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Domain personas use relevance gates. An image verifier can correctly no-op on non-image work; a Python verifier can no-op on non-Python work. Match the recipe to the task.</p></div></article>
+          <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>FAILED / no oracle</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Do not fight the result. Preserve the gap, identify the missing fixture or checker capability, and use the flywheel option.</p></div></article>
+          <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Model unavailable</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>The shipped personas currently name <code>openai/gpt-5.5</code>, but runtime availability was not verified in this chapter build. Use a provider/model you can access and record the front-matter change.</p></div></article>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">12</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Close the chapter and retrieve the system from first principles. Score evidence and boundaries, not vocabulary alone.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round 1 · Architecture from memory</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative architecture retrieval">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Draw the path from genuine user prompt to the next verified builder state.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>User prompts builder → builder captures original prompt and start line → builder completes → <code>agent_end</code> emits stop metadata with end line → verifier renders <code>verify_on_stop.md</code> → reads bounded JSONL slice → decomposes and checks atoms → sends Report; on actionable failure it also uses <code>verifier_prompt</code> → builder injects correction as follow-up → another stop triggers another verification cycle.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does and does not cross the socket?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The socket carries lifecycle events with original prompt and line offsets, connection/control messages, acknowledgements, corrections, Reports, and liveness/bye messages. It does not stream the builder assistant transcript; the verifier reads that turn from session JSONL on disk.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 2 · Diagnose the evidence</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative evidence retrieval">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">A Report says “database valid” because <code>PRAGMA integrity_check</code> returned <code>ok</code>. What is missing?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Integrity alone does not prove requested primary keys, foreign-key targets, columns, defaults, or indexes. Those claims need separate atoms and checks.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">An image verifier says a diagram has seven text blocks. What kind of evidence is that?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A model-mediated visual judgment grounded in the actual image, not a deterministic parser result.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 3 · Diagnose the authority</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative security retrieval">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the intended SQLite layers and one caveat for each architecture claim.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Persona tool list omits write/edit, but bash remains. The tool-call hook narrows bash, but currently uses a string-prefix predicate. The SQLite script rejects mutation SQL, allowlists PRAGMAs, and opens read-only, but forwards dot-commands through <code>sqlite3 -readonly</code> without its own dot-command allowlist. The process still runs as the user.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why can’t the same “three layers” claim be applied to every persona?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Generic and image personas do not declare the script-only bash policy; Python runs checkers/tests that may have side effects. Authority is domain-specific.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 4 · Explain the economics</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative economics retrieval">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Resolve the paradox: how can 2–5× the tokens make delivery faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The second stream of compute performs claim decomposition, review checks, correction, and re-checking that the human would otherwise do serially. The comparison is against accountable reviewed work, not unreviewed builder output.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why “AND, not OR”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The system composes a builder role and verifier role, potentially with different models and evidence surfaces. The pattern shifts attention from selecting one isolated winner to orchestrating specialized roles. Different models are a hypothesis to evaluate, not an automatic guarantee.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <div class="teach-back">
+          <h3>Capstone teach-back · four minutes, no notes</h3>
+          <p>Explain: the planning/reviewing framework; the observer lifecycle and socket nuance; atomic claims and domain-appropriate evidence; the token-for-time exchange; why trust and scale can rise together; the two input locks; the scoped SQLite authority layers and current gaps; the Report flywheel; and why stack intelligence requires harness control.</p>
+          <p class="fine"><strong>Mastery standard:</strong> a listener could reproduce the course lab, avoid overclaiming security, and diagnose the next verifier miss into context, model, prompt, or tools.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Glossary</h2>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>Atomic claim</dt><dd>The smallest independently checkable proposition extracted from the request or builder result.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Builder</dt><dd>The interactive Pi process that performs the user’s work. Its harness owns the verifier connection; its model does not delegate verification.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Verifier</dt><dd>The sibling observer process that starts a verification cycle after builder completion and checks work against domain evidence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Top-down observer</dt><dd>A verifier initiated by lifecycle hooks outside the builder model’s task flow rather than by model delegation.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Session JSONL slice</dt><dd>The bounded range of builder session events for one turn, identified by start/end line offsets in lifecycle metadata.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>verifier_prompt</code></dt><dd>The verifier’s typed outbound correction tool; it sends a prompt envelope and awaits acknowledgement from the builder.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Planning constraint</dt><dd>Dan’s term for the human capacity to define intent, sequence, boundaries, and success evidence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Review constraint</dt><dd>Dan’s term for the human capacity to check generated work against actual state. The verifier directly targets this constraint.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Domain-appropriate evidence</dt><dd>The observation that settles an atom in its domain: deterministic query/test output where available, or model-mediated inspection grounded in the artifact for visual claims.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>PERFECT</dt><dd>Every atomic claim checked, no gaps, and no corrective prompt needed in the cycle.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>FEEDBACK</dt><dd>At least one atom failed and the verifier sent a concrete correction; the loop is functioning.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>FAILED</dt><dd>The verifier could not perform the required verification because an oracle, fixture, command, or clarity was missing.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Templated engineering</dt><dd>Encoding a recurring verification improvement into the persona, checker script, or cycle template rather than a one-off chat instruction.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Verifier flywheel</dt><dd>The loop from explicit unverifiable gap to a new durable evidence capability and a rerun that closes the gap.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Read-only by architecture</dt><dd>The design practice of removing mutation tools and narrowing execution outside the model. In this course, guarantees must be scoped and audited per persona.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Script-prefix gate</dt><dd>The current SQLite bash policy implementation: allow command strings beginning with the configured runner and script. It narrows bash but is weaker than parsed argv or fixed executable dispatch.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Stack intelligence</dt><dd>Combine specialized model roles inside a controlled system—builder AND verifier—rather than treating one-model selection as the entire problem.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Core Four</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools: the durable surfaces to improve when the verifier misses.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt><code>max_loops</code></dt><dd>The per-persona automatic correction limit. The course default is three; the next attempted correction escalates to the human.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Same-user filesystem authorization</dt><dd>The socket directory’s <code>0700</code> permission prevents other UIDs from traversing it; it is not cryptographic peer authentication.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+
+        <p class="source-note">Primary sources: MA-4 transcript and lesson extract; <code>agentic-horizon-extras/the-verifier-agent-system</code> course files; twelve extracted 3840×2160 lesson frames. Runtime model availability, paid-provider authentication, external video metadata, and live end-to-end execution were not asserted by this static chapter build.</p>
+      </section>
+
